.TH READ_LINE 3ggl "9 August 1997" "GGL/IGP ETHZ" "GGL Common Lib"
.SH NAME
read_line, read_delim \- read an array of char upto a newline or delimiter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <common/inout.h>

.RB "int " read_line
.RI "(FILE *" fpin ", char **" lineptr ");"

.RB "int " read_delim
.RI "(FILE *" fpin ", int " delimiter ", char **" lineptr ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The function 
.B read_delim()
reads bytes from a stream specified by
.I fpin
until it finds a character identical to
.I delimiter
or until it reaches end of file.
The function
.B read_line()
assumes the delimiter to be a newline character and thus, no
.I delimiter
argument may be passed to it.
The pointer to the newly allocated string of read characters
is stored into the location passed by
.IR lineptr .

When using
.B read_line()
the current line
up to and including the newline character is stored
into a newly allocated string.

With
.BR read_delim() ,
the current line
up to and including the 
.I delimiter
character is stored
into a newly allocated string.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.BR read_delim() " and " read_line()
return the size of this newly allocated string containing
the read information, unless an error occurred while
reading from the file. In case of an error or upon reaching
the end of file,
.BR read_delim() " and " read_line()
return
.BR EOF .

The pointer to the newly allocated string is stored into the
location passed by
.IR lineptr .
.SH ERRORS
The functions will call standard library functions
that may set
.IR errno .
If any called function produces an error, or if
the end of file is reached, the return value will be
set to
.BR EOF .
.SH BUGS
The Solaris version of read_delim is only a quick workaround
that does not behave as described above. This is because
the GNU extension `getdelim' is not avaliable on that platform.
Somebody want to code it out platform independently?
.SH CAVEATS
You have to compile your program using
.I -lcommon
as a compiler flag to properly link
your function to the library.
